{"authored" [{"question" "q-330826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/review.json" "file_sha256" "0b2fdfa341c1c85c41df02e02f3f88a934f24b8115202a4263ce4164a38f2f23" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "8c39ef497c1c8847905b6784297c6a42f052318ff6306c67e9283f704104e60d" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/verification.json" "basis" 2700 "rationale" "Only the final convergence domain is erroneous. Direct integration uniquely gives the original displayed option a on the exact maximal real domain s>0. The canonical integral rule supports the same formula; original source grade must be observed separately."} {"question" "q-330826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluate the integral first:\n\n$$g(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-3t}}3.$$\n\nFor $s>0$, both exponential integrals converge, and\n\n$$\\mathcal L\\{g\\}(s)=\\frac13\\left(\\frac1s-\\frac1{s+3}\\right)=\\frac1{s(s+3)}.$$\n\nThis is option a. The integrand $g(t)$ tends to $1/3$. In fact, for $t\\ge1$, $g(t)\\ge(1-e^{-3})/3>0$, so the defining Laplace integral diverges for every real $s\\le0$. Thus the maximal real convergence domain is $s>0$. The original source's $s>-3$ is the convergence condition for $e^{-3t}$, not for its integral; extending the rational expression to negative $s$ does not extend the transform." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/review.json" "file_sha256" "0b2fdfa341c1c85c41df02e02f3f88a934f24b8115202a4263ce4164a38f2f23" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "8c39ef497c1c8847905b6784297c6a42f052318ff6306c67e9283f704104e60d" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/verification.json" "basis" 2700 "rationale" "Only the final convergence domain is erroneous. Direct integration uniquely gives the original displayed option a on the exact maximal real domain s>0. The canonical integral rule supports the same formula; original source grade must be observed separately."} {"question" "q-330826" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/review.json" "file_sha256" "0b2fdfa341c1c85c41df02e02f3f88a934f24b8115202a4263ce4164a38f2f23" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "8c39ef497c1c8847905b6784297c6a42f052318ff6306c67e9283f704104e60d" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-330826/verification.json" "basis" 2700 "rationale" "Only the final convergence domain is erroneous. Direct integration uniquely gives the original displayed option a on the exact maximal real domain s>0. The canonical integral rule supports the same formula; original source grade must be observed separately."} {"question" "q-330826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "427904aa631f3ab0eccfb0df61d246c2416475a9743045f7134f7324e6bd0c68" "basis" 2699 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-330826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that if $L{f(t)}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{-3t}.$ Thus, for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L{f(t)} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>-3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{g(t)} & =L{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "427904aa631f3ab0eccfb0df61d246c2416475a9743045f7134f7324e6bd0c68" "basis" 2699 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-330826" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "279c2a1df0bdee3c31bfce5fcb8522ea73c02ce8cfebfcb28687ef2b1bf9da9c" "basis" 2699 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-330826" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073261/state.json" "file_sha256" "